  "summary": "Keys 1, 2 and 3 now select the glass, metal and fabric sphere. The selected one spins about its vertical axis at 0.6 rad/s, and the others keep their current angle. `window.__TASK_STATE__.selected` is `'glass' | 'metal' | 'fabric' | null` and starts as `null`. Numpad 1–3 work too. `typecheck` passes and the final `render` is error-free with the same layout as before. I did not press any keys in the render, so the spin and `selected` updates are untested at runtime.\n\nThe spheres used to be nodes inside one bridged model, and Aura3D only lets you set node transforms at load time. So I split the recipe with a `part` param (`floor`, `glass`, `metal`, `fabric`) and ran `import_three` four times. The floor is now the `studio` model, and each sphere is its own bridged model with its own runtime id (`sphere-glass`, `sphere-metal`, `sphere-fabric`). That lets `main.ts` rotate one sphere without moving the others. Geometry and materials are unchanged.\n\nThe metal sphere's anisotropic reflection shifts slightly as it spins. The glass is smooth, so its spin is barely visible."
